<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lament · c. 1960 · Sub-Saharan Africa</p><h1 class="sg-title">Tizita</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="am">ትዝታ</bdi> / <bdi lang="am-Latn">Tizita</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>Tizita means &quot;memory&quot; or &quot;longing&quot;: a kind of Ethiopian song, made by village musicians, about missing people and times gone by.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (the form developed among Amhara village musicians, the azmaris)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Amhara peasantry and azmari musicians; later Ethiopians and Eritreans in general</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Amharic</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Amhara countryside, Ethiopia</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>not known (traditional; Ethio-jazz built on it from the 1960s)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (a traditional song form and mode, not one text; modern tizita songs have their own authors)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>Tizita (also Tezeta; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a> ትዝታ) means memory, nostalgia or longing. The word names one of the qenet, the pentatonic scales or modes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, and the songs sung in it. The term belongs to Amharic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigrinya-language">Tigrinya</a> and has no equivalent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geez">Ge'ez</a>. Tizita folk songs developed in the countryside among the Amhara peasantry and the village musicians called azmaris.</p><p>Tizita songs became a popular genre in both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, named after the mode in which they are sung, and are known for moving listeners deeply, among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> and among a great many other Ethiopians. Western writers often compare tizita to the blues; other comparisons are the Portuguese saudade, the assouf of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a>, the Romanian dor and the German Sehnsucht.</p><p>In the 1960s the tizita mode became one of the foundations of Ethiopian jazz, or Ethio-jazz, which joined it with elements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-orthodox-church">Ethiopian Orthodox</a> Christian music and with Western instruments; Mulatu Astatke was its pioneer. Modern Ethiopian musicians who have performed tizita songs include Aster Aweke, Hailu Mergia, Bezawork Asfaw, Teddy Afro, Mulatu Astatke, Meklit Hadero, Seyfu Yohannes and Mahmoud Ahmed.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>Tizita is less one song than a mood and a musical mode: songs of remembrance and longing for what has been lost, whether a person, a place or a past time. The mode itself carries the feeling, so that listeners recognise the ache of memory before the words.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords" data-say>The words of this song are in the public domain, but no free, reliable edition of them has been found yet to import them from, and the Institute never types a song&#8217;s words from memory. &#8220;What it says&#8221; above tells what they are about.</p></section>
</div>
